<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-page1-subgame-perfection" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Subgame Perfect Nash Equilibria</title>

  <p>
    Back in Section 3.7, we found three Nash equilibria of Big Monkey and
    Little Monkey: <m>(w, cw)</m>, <m>(c, ww)</m>, and <m>(w, cc)</m>. In
    this chapter, we'll see a stronger version of Nash equilibria.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-1">
    <title>Section 6.1, Subgame Perfect Nash Equilibria</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This section is short but dense, since it's mostly definitions. We'll
      work through them with an example right after.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Part of the definition of a subgame deals with information sets, which
    only matter in games with incomplete information. We'll only be focusing on games
    with complete information, which makes things a bit simpler!
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-spne">
    <title>Subgame Perfect Nash Equilibrium</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A strategy profile is a <term>subgame perfect Nash equilibrium</term>
        if, for every subgame, the restriction of the strategy profile to
        that subgame is a Nash equilibrium of that subgame.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Notice that when we look at a subgame, moves at nodes outside the
    subgame are disregarded in the restricted strategies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the game tree for Big Monkey and Little Monkey, with Big Monkey
    moving first. We've labeled Little Monkey's two nodes <m>n_1</m> (after
    Big Monkey waits) and <m>n_2</m> (after Big Monkey climbs).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bmlm-tree-labeled">
    <caption>Big Monkey and Little Monkey. Payoffs are listed as (Big Monkey, Little Monkey).</caption>
    <image source="ch6-graph/bmlm-tree.svg" width="85%">
      <shortdescription>Game tree for Big Monkey and Little Monkey</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          At the root, Big Monkey chooses wait or climb. If Big Monkey waits,
          Little Monkey chooses at node <m>n_1</m>: wait gives payoffs
          <m>(0, 0)</m>, and climb gives <m>(9, 1)</m>. If Big Monkey climbs,
          Little Monkey chooses at node <m>n_2</m>: wait gives
          <m>(4, 4)</m>, and climb gives <m>(5, 3)</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bmlm-number-of-subgames">
    <statement>
      <p>How many subgames does this game have?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>2</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close! You probably counted the subgames starting at <m>n_1</m>
            and <m>n_2</m>. The whole game, which starts at the root, also
            counts as a subgame.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>3</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. The three subgames are:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>the subgame starting at the root (the whole game),</p></li>
            <li><p>the subgame starting at <m>n_1</m> (after Big Monkey waits), and</p></li>
            <li><p>the subgame starting at <m>n_2</m> (after Big Monkey climbs).</p></li>
          </ol>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- (w, cw): worked example                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Now we'll use the widget below to explain why <m>(w, cw)</m> is a
    subgame perfect Nash equilibrium. The moves of <m>(w, cw)</m> are drawn
    thick. Click each of the three nodes to see the subgame that starts
    there, what <m>(w, cw)</m> restricts to in that subgame, and whether
    the restriction is a Nash equilibrium.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bmlm-subgame-explorer">
    <caption>The subgames of Big Monkey and Little Monkey, and the strategy profile <m>(w, cw)</m>.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-bmlm-subgame-explorer"
      iframe="ch6-graph/bmlm-subgame-explorer.html" width="100%" aspect="1:1">
      <static>
        <p>
          The Big Monkey and Little Monkey tree with clickable nodes. For
          each subgame, the widget lists each player's strategies in the
          subgame, shows the original normal form with impossible outcomes
          crossed out, and shows the smaller game the subgame really is.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Root: the whole game. The profile is <m>(w, cw)</m>, a Nash
              equilibrium, since Big Monkey gets 9 from <m>w</m> versus 4
              from <m>c</m>, and <m>cw</m> gives Little Monkey her best
              payoff, 1, in row <m>w</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>n_1</m>: Big Monkey has no strategies, and Little Monkey
              chooses <m>w</m> for <m>(0, 0)</m> or <m>c</m> for
              <m>(9, 1)</m>. <m>cw</m> restricts to <m>c</m>, a Nash
              equilibrium, since <m>1 \geq 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>n_2</m>: Big Monkey has no strategies, and Little Monkey
              chooses <m>w</m> for <m>(4, 4)</m> or <m>c</m> for
              <m>(5, 3)</m>. <m>cw</m> restricts to <m>w</m>, a Nash
              equilibrium, since <m>4 \geq 3</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We showed that in each of the three subgames, the restriction of
    <m>(w, cw)</m> to the subgame is a Nash equilibrium of that subgame. So
    <m>(w, cw)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- (c, ww): students do it                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    Now it's your turn to do a similar analysis, but with the strategy profile <m>(c, ww)</m>! On the tree below,
    we've thickened the branches that correspond to the moves described with this strategy profile.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bmlm-tree-c-ww">
    <caption>The strategy profile <m>(c, ww)</m>.</caption>
    <image source="ch6-graph/bmlm-tree-c-ww.svg" width="85%">
      <shortdescription>Game tree for Big Monkey and Little Monkey with the profile (c, ww)</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          The Big Monkey and Little Monkey tree, with the moves of
          <m>(c, ww)</m> drawn thick: Big Monkey climbs at the root, Little
          Monkey waits at <m>n_1</m>, and Little Monkey waits at
          <m>n_2</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-build-subgame-n1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the subgame starting at <m>n_1</m>, after Big Monkey waits.
        Click every outcome in the original normal form below that is still
        possible in this subgame.
      </p>

      <p>Be careful! This subgame is the node that starts after Big Monkey waits, even though in the original strategy profile, <m>(c, ww)</m>, Big Monkey
      climbs!! </p>
    </statement>
    <areas>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell></cell>
          <cell>LM: ww</cell>
          <cell>LM: wc</cell>
          <cell>LM: cw</cell>
          <cell>LM: cc</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>BM: w</cell>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(0, 0)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(0, 0)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(9, 1)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(9, 1)</area></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>BM: c</cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(4, 4)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(5, 3)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(4, 4)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(5, 3)</area></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </areas>
    <hint>
      <p>
        In this subgame, Big Monkey has already made his move. Which row
        does that leave?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The whole <m>w</m> row. Big Monkey has already waited, so the
        outcomes in the <m>c</m> row can't happen. In the <m>w</m> row,
        only the first letter of Little Monkey's strategy matters, so
        <m>ww</m> and <m>wc</m> both act like <m>w</m>, and <m>cw</m> and
        <m>cc</m> both act like <m>c</m>. That makes the subgame at
        <m>n_1</m> really this game, where Big Monkey has no strategies:
      </p>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>LM: w</cell>
          <cell>LM: c</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>(9, 1)</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So the subgame at <m>n_1</m> is really the smaller game below. Big
    Monkey has no strategies in this subgame, and Little Monkey just chooses
    <m>w</m> or <m>c</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-outcome-c-ww-n1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the subgame at <m>n_1</m>, click the outcome that the restriction
        of <m>(c, ww)</m> leads to.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <areas>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>LM: w</cell>
          <cell>LM: c</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(0, 0)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(9, 1)</area></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </areas>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Which letter of <m>ww</m> tells Little Monkey what to do at
        <m>n_1</m>?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>(0, 0)</m>. Only the first letter of <m>ww</m> matters at
        <m>n_1</m>, so <m>ww</m> restricts to <m>w</m>: Little Monkey waits.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-outcome-c-ww-n2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The subgame at <m>n_2</m>, after Big Monkey climbs, is really the
        smaller game below. Again, Big Monkey has no strategies in this
        subgame. Click the outcome that the restriction of <m>(c, ww)</m>
        leads to.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <areas>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col right="minor"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>LM: w</cell>
          <cell>LM: c</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><area correct="yes">(4, 4)</area></cell>
          <cell><area correct="no">(5, 3)</area></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </areas>
    <hint>
      <p>
        This time, which letter of <m>ww</m> tells Little Monkey what to do?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>(4, 4)</m>. Only the second letter of <m>ww</m> matters at
        <m>n_2</m>, so <m>ww</m> restricts to <m>w</m> again: Little Monkey
        waits.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Notice what happened in the subgame at <m>n_1</m>. Here is its table
    again, with the outcome of the restricted strategy profile boxed:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-c-ww-n1-outcome">
    <caption>The subgame at <m>n_1</m>, with the outcome of <m>(c, ww)</m> restricted to it boxed.</caption>
    <image source="ch6-graph/subgame-n1-c-ww.svg" width="35%">
      <shortdescription>The subgame at n1 with the outcome (0, 0) boxed</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A one-row table for the subgame at <m>n_1</m>. Little Monkey's
          strategy <m>w</m> gives the outcome <m>(0, 0)</m>, which is boxed,
          since it is the outcome of <m>(c, ww)</m> restricted to this
          subgame. Little Monkey's strategy <m>c</m> gives <m>(9, 1)</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Little Monkey waits and gets 0, but climbing would give her 1. So the
    restriction of <m>(c, ww)</m> to this subgame is <em>not</em> a Nash
    equilibrium! (Notice that it is a Nash equilibrium in the other two subgames.) That
    means <m>(c, ww)</m> is <em>not</em> a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall that Little Monkey's plan to wait if Big Monkey waits is a <term>threat</term>
    (it would hurt Big Monkey, but it would
    also hurt Little Monkey). However, <m>(c, ww)</m> is still a Nash equilibrium of
    the whole game, because Big Monkey climbs, so <m>n_1</m> is never
    reached and Little Monkey never has to carry out the threat! Checking whether the Nash equilibrium is subgame
    perfect catches this because we check every subgame. The threat only works if Big Monkey believes the threat. If they don't believe the threat,
    they will go ahead and climb, forcing Little Monkey to decide whether they want to make the rational move or the threat! 
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.2 and (w, cc)                                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-2">
    <title>Section 6.2, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 6</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You've already seen most of this section! It covers the same three
      Nash equilibria, and it also explains the third one,
      <m>(w, cc)</m>. It is a good idea to quickly think about <m>(w, cc)</m> on your own first before reading!
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-w-cc-subgame">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>(w, cc)</m> is a Nash equilibrium of the whole game, but it is
        not subgame perfect. In which subgame is its restriction not a Nash
        equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p>The subgame at <m>n_1</m>, after Big Monkey waits.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. At <m>n_1</m>, <m>cc</m> restricts to <m>c</m>, and
            climbing gives Little Monkey 1, which is better than the 0 she'd
            get from waiting. So that restriction is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>The subgame at <m>n_2</m>, after Big Monkey climbs.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. At <m>n_2</m>, <m>cc</m> restricts to <m>c</m>, and
            climbing gives Little Monkey 3, but waiting would give her 4.
            Her plan to climb if Big Monkey climbs is a
            <term>promise</term>: it would help Big Monkey, at a cost to
            Little Monkey.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- 6.3                                                          -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-6-3">
    <title>Section 6.3, Subgame Perfect Equilibria and Backward Induction</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 6.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The main point is in the first paragraph: backward induction finds
      subgame perfect Nash equilibria, because it chooses the best move at
      every node, whether or not that node is reached.</p>
      
      <p> Notice that
      <m>(w, cw)</m>, the subgame perfect equilibrium we found earlier, is
      exactly the backward induction answer from Chapter 1. 
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Now you might be wondering why we care about subgame perfect Nash equilibria if they just
    correspond to the backward induction outcome. Actually, this is only
    true for finite games, where the game tree has finitely many nodes, so
    there is a last move to start backward induction from. The last
    paragraph of Section 6.3 goes into this. In games that go
    on forever, there's no last move to start backward induction from, but
    we can still start the game at any point and ask whether the players'
    plans make sense from there. That's how we'll use subgame perfection
    for repeated games on the next page.
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>Skip Sections 6.4 and 6.5.</alert> We'll pick up again at Section 6.6.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-6-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=6.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
